 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time and Memory Complexity:** Memory efficiency is maintained through adaptive spatio-temporal densification, which limits the number of Gaussians and keyframes [S1]. The method uses modified Adam optimization for fast optimization and rendering [S1].
-- **Measured Performance:** Experiments were conducted on an NVIDIA A100 GPU with 40GB of VRAM [S1]. Evaluation metrics include PSNR, SSIM, LPIPS, and VMAF [LLLK13] (which accounts for cross-frame consistency) [S1].
-- **Scaling:** The method is designed for large scenes with complex visibility, where other methods like 4D-GS densification threshold tuning fail to increase primitive count or quality due to sparse gradients and suboptimal Gaussian placement [S1].
-- **Strengths and Limitations:** Strengths include memory efficiency, fast optimization, and no requirement for user interaction or prior-based supervision [S1]. Limitations include the complexity of error moment computation and the need for calibrated input images [S1].
+*   **Time and Memory Complexity:** The method maintains memory efficiency through adaptive spatio-temporal densification, adding Gaussians and keyframes only where needed [S1]. The use of piecewise splines for displacement avoids storing per-frame displacements for all Gaussians [S1].
+*   **Measured Performance:** Evaluated on the AmbientGaussians dataset [S1]. For the "Forest Scene #1" (large scene), it achieves a PSNR of 24.24, SSIM of 0.82, LPIPS of 0.19, and VMAF of 70.36, with 2.02M Gaussians and a training time of 54.17 minutes on an NVIDIA A100 GPU [S1]. For "Forest Scene #4", it achieves 20.58 PSNR, 0.30 LPIPS, and 60.03 VMAF with 3.58M Gaussians and 1h 10m training time [S1].
+*   **Scaling:** The method is designed for large scenes with complex visibility, where other methods like 4D-GS fail to increase primitive count or quality due to sparse gradients [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Fast optimization and rendering, no user interaction or prior-based supervision required, handles oscillatory motion well [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Relies on calibrated input (SfM). The splitting strategy is guided by error moments, which may require careful tuning of $\tau_{\text{space}}$ [S1].
+*   **When to use:** Reconstruction of dynamic scenes from monocular video with non-rigid motion (e.g., foliage) [S1].
+*   **When not to use:** Scenes requiring explicit surface mesh extraction without additional normal regularization , or scenes with static geometry where standard 3DGS is sufficient.
 
 ## Background
-Adaptive Spatio-Temporal 3D Gaussian Splatting was introduced to address the impracticality of manual segmentation for dynamic elements in monocular videos, such as leaves [S1]. It builds on the 3D Gaussian Splatting framework [KKLD23] and adapts it for dynamic scenes by introducing piecewise spline representations for motion and error-moment-guided densification [S1]. The method was developed to enable reconstruction of dynamic scenes from monocular video using an explicit representation that maintains memory efficiency [S1].
+Adaptive Spatio-Temporal 3D Gaussian Splatting was introduced in the paper "Adaptive Spatio‐Temporal 3D Gaussian Splatting for Scenes with Oscillatory Motion" [S1]. It builds on the 3D Gaussian Splatting framework [KKLD23] and addresses limitations in existing dynamic scene reconstruction methods that require manual segmentation or prior-based supervision [S1]. The method was developed to handle scenes with large amounts of motion, such as leaves oscillating in the wind, using an explicit representation that maintains memory efficiency [S1].
 
